90
00:06:51,960 --> 00:06:54,091
Not a bad way, I think, to start the conference.

91
00:07:01,050 --> 00:07:03,930
Our logo is a smiling troll,

92
00:07:05,550 --> 00:07:09,660
which takes us from Munch to Norse mythology,

93
00:07:11,550 --> 00:07:14,790
and to something surprisingly close to what we're working on.

94
00:07:15,930 --> 00:07:21,780
In old Norse sources, trolls dwell in the wild,

95
00:07:21,930 --> 00:07:22,920
isolated places,

96
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:27,030
mountains, rocks, caves, forests.

97
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:31,860
They live in small family groups, and they're rarely helpful to humans.

98
00:07:33,870 --> 00:07:37,081
They belong to nature, and they're out at the edge of the known world.

99
00:07:41,701 --> 00:07:42,540
And here we see

100
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:45,901
Kittelsen and the Norwegian troll.

101
00:07:46,350 --> 00:07:52,081
For over a century, our mental image of the Norwegian troll has been that

102
00:07:52,770 --> 00:07:58,320
created by Theodor Kittelsen. He illustrated the folk tales of

103
00:07:58,410 --> 00:08:04,230
Asbjørnsen and Moe for nearly 30 years, and many say that he

104
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:07,800
settled once and for all what a Norwegian troll looks like.

105
00:08:10,680 --> 00:08:11,701
And it's that connection

106
00:08:12,990 --> 00:08:18,300
that I want to draw here again to TADWIG, because before Kittelsen, the troll

107
00:08:18,841 --> 00:08:22,800
was in a way formless. Everyone pictured it differently.

108
00:08:24,390 --> 00:08:28,651
But he gave it a shared form. He gave it a kind of common language. Right?

109
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:31,620
And this is exactly I would say that we're doing here at TADWIG.

110
00:08:32,341 --> 00:08:38,190
Biodiversity, like a troll, is wild, scattered, endlessly varied, and hard to

111
00:08:38,220 --> 00:08:42,961
pin down. Our task here as a group is to give it a shared language,

112
00:08:44,401 --> 00:08:50,340
standards that let data from a forest in Uganda and a specimen drawer

113
00:08:50,401 --> 00:08:51,061
here in Oslo

114
00:08:52,350 --> 00:08:53,370
speak to one another.

115
00:08:55,410 --> 00:08:58,110
We in Norway like to think we live close to nature.

116
00:08:58,651 --> 00:09:02,490
We build our stories around it, and data standards are simply

117
00:09:03,931 --> 00:09:09,120
how we turn that wild, tangled diversity into something we can share, connect, and

118
00:09:09,151 --> 00:09:09,390
protect.

119
00:09:13,152 --> 00:09:17,592
So let us go from trolls to living nature.

120
00:09:18,642 --> 00:09:24,192
The image here on the screen is the University of Oslo's Botanical Garden, Norway's

121
00:09:24,252 --> 00:09:29,652
oldest. It was founded in 1814 and is part of the Natural History Museum.

122
00:09:30,402 --> 00:09:36,012
We have around 5,000 plant species cared for through research, education,

123
00:09:36,852 --> 00:09:41,412
and conservation. The conference itself is here and also at the Congress Center,

124
00:09:42,072 --> 00:09:46,633
but as a host, I would like you to also enjoy the botanical garden.

125
00:09:46,722 --> 00:09:51,912
So if you have time, please do come and explore the wonderful garden while you're

126
00:09:51,942 --> 00:09:52,242
