00:32:30,546 --> 00:32:34,207
We need to think carefully about which path we take to serve our growing community.

421
00:32:34,656 --> 00:32:40,177
So I'd like to take the next couple of minutes and explain how we got here, and

422
00:32:42,336 --> 00:32:48,246
it's my hope that what we can do is find a way to help you help us

423
00:32:48,336 --> 00:32:49,627
to stay on the right path.

424
00:32:52,927 --> 00:32:58,116
So in the 1980s, when TDWG was founded, that's a photograph from the 1980s.

425
00:32:59,106 --> 00:33:02,076
The purpose was to explore taxonomy.

426
00:33:02,136 --> 00:33:07,927
That was the primary focus, and understandably, at the time, participants tended to

427
00:33:07,957 --> 00:33:12,427
be taxonomists, mostly botanists, and academics interested in names.

428
00:33:13,207 --> 00:33:18,276
At the time, communication was limited to handwritten or typed correspondence and

429
00:33:18,306 --> 00:33:19,836
through the mail, I know,

430
00:33:22,506 --> 00:33:27,756
and telephone calls. And the TDWG meetings, such as they were, tended to be the

431
00:33:27,846 --> 00:33:32,317
only opportunity for our community to get together to discuss work in person.

432
00:33:32,856 --> 00:33:37,806
At the time, telephones were just as likely to be used as weapons of murder in

433
00:33:37,866 --> 00:33:42,786
Alfred Hitchcock films as much as they were for communication, and the desktop

434
00:33:42,817 --> 00:33:47,076
machines that we were using at the time were truly personal computers.

435
00:33:48,276 --> 00:33:53,346
The hot topics of discussion, hold onto your seat, were the isolated relational

436
00:33:53,405 --> 00:33:57,216
databases that people were learning how to develop at the time.

437
00:33:59,226 --> 00:34:00,756
Then one day, lightning struck,

438
00:34:02,106 --> 00:34:05,136
and we found ourselves on the cusp of something revolutionary.

439
00:34:05,796 --> 00:34:10,056
So this is a paragraph from some correspondence that TDWG's deputy chair, Eli

440
00:34:10,116 --> 00:34:14,826
Saliba, discovered in a box under a cavernous desk at a botanic garden somewhere in

441
00:34:14,886 --> 00:34:17,826
Belgium while practicing his Indiana Jones cosplay.

442
00:34:23,317 --> 00:34:28,266
The good news is that TDWG, this person, and others understood the value of the

443
00:34:28,326 --> 00:34:33,067
witchcraft that we now know to be the internet, and soon afterward, the next age of

444
00:34:33,127 --> 00:34:34,236
TDWG was upon us.

445
00:34:36,396 --> 00:34:42,067
So TDWG pivoted, and the focus became primary biodiversity data or occurrence data

446
00:34:42,187 --> 00:34:45,427
around the turn of the century. It even sounds old when we say around the turn of

447
00:34:45,457 --> 00:34:50,437
the century. It was a time when those data that were living in isolated relational

448
00:34:50,496 --> 00:34:54,246
databases started to come of age and wanted to get out of the house and away from

449
00:34:54,276 --> 00:34:59,376
their parents. Soon, those data found data portals such as GBIF and the Vertebrate

450
00:34:59,436 --> 00:35:03,996
Networks, both of which I will add are celebrating their 25th anniversaries this

451
00:35:04,056 --> 00:35:09,726
year. Those portals then evolved into places where the data could hang out,

452
00:35:10,056 --> 00:35:11,826
socialize, and be themselves.

453
00:35:13,236 --> 00:35:18,427
New communities of practice arrived at the party, so new tools, formats, and

454
00:35:18,486 --> 00:35:20,346
standards were needed to meet that demand.

455
00:35:20,976 --> 00:35:26,916
XML, RDF, data schemas were all applied, and data standards such as

456
00:35:26,976 --> 00:35:32,586
ABCD and Darwin Core were born. A new focus on ontology to standardize vocabularies

457
00:35:32,646 --> 00:35:38,286
blossomed, and new telecommunications tools like Skype or Zoom changed the way that

458
00:35:38,317 --> 00:35:42,636
we work together, and even our annual meetings begin to evolve into joint ventures

459
